{
 "id": "2021-wu-urban",
 "name": "Urban Landscape Forest Elements: Restoration and Preference (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Wu, L., Dong, Q., Luo, S., Jiang, W., Hao, M., & Chen, Q. (2021). Effects of spatial elements of urban landscape forests on the restoration potential and preference of adolescents. Land 10:1349. The 18 stimulus images of the paper's Figure 1: urban transportation and a city square (group A), a photograph of an urban ginkgo forest in autumn (B), and the same forest edited to add terrain, water, flowers and shrubs, one element at a time (C), in pairs (D), in threes (E) and all four together (F). After gender and age, participants see the images one at a time in random order; each is shown alone for 15 seconds and then rated on the six-item Restoration Outcome Scale (I feel restored and relaxed; I feel calm; I have energy for my everyday routines; I feel focused and alert; I can forget everyday worries; my thoughts are clear; 1 = very unlikely … 7 = very likely) and on preference (how much do you like this scene or environment; 0 = I do not like it at all … 4 = I like it very much). Analysis as in the paper: restoration value = mean of the six ROS items; stepwise linear regression of restoration and preference on dummy variables for terrain, water, flowers and shrubs. Original result (120 students aged 18–25 in Chengdu): the city scenes scored lowest on restoration, forest + water + terrain and forest + water + terrain + shrubs highest; forest + water alone was liked most; water was the only element that significantly predicted restoration and preference.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) only the second part (image ratings) is replicated; the first part (a stress task, 5 minutes viewing the ginkgo forest, blood pressure, heart rate, blood oxygen, STAI-S anxiety and ROS before and after) is left out. (2) Images are shown in random order instead of the fixed group order. (3) The paper shows each image for 15 s and lets participants score it; here the questions appear after the 15 s, with the image still shown. (4) The paper prints the fifth ROS item as \"I can forget every day words\"; we use the standard wording \"I can forget everyday worries\". (5) The question lead-in (\"Imagine you are in this scene…\") is ours. (6) Images are cut from the published figure (about 470 × 350 px).\n\nImages: Figure 1 of the paper (CC BY 4.0), used as published with credit to the authors; the template is removed if the authors object.",
